 "verdict": "PRIMARY, the registered arm (subadd on the SETTLED ensemble): rho = +0.270 (p = 0.3484, n = 14), against T*'s +0.547 on the same target and models. Secondary (spread, settled): -0.310 (p = 0.2822). EXPLORATORY, and reported only so the arm-shopping is visible rather than hidden: the random-ensemble arms give subadd +0.552 (p=0.0445), spread -0.015 (p=0.9622). The best of these exceeds T* by 0.005, which is not a result -- it is what picking the largest of four correlations looks like, and F96's regime work says the settled ensemble is the one that governs the dynamics anyway. DEFLATION CHECK (frozen before the run): rho(subadd, T*) = +0.900 (p = 0.0833, n = 5). The index tracks T* closely, so it is largely a restatement of a metric the project already has rather than a new one. The registered index does NOT beat T* (+0.270 against +0.547, p = 0.3484). Since T* is cheaper and already validated, the canalization route does not add an external predictor and closes as designed -- the registered kill, not a disappointment. AND THE INDEX ITSELF IS NEAR ZERO ON EVERY REAL MODEL: sub-additivity spans +0.0033 to +0.0279, against F96's validated ladder where Domany-Kinzel's p2=0 line sits at +0.960, ECA rule 150 (XOR) at +1.000 and rule 232 (majority) at +0.250. Every model is two orders of magnitude below the weakest anchor, i.e. the two-token response is essentially ADDITIVE and nowhere near canalizing. That is a substantive negative about what these local functions are, and it is independent of whether the index predicts anything. BOUNDARY: greedy-scoped target, one radius, one temperature, and the models are not independent draws -- F86 states its own anchor at family level for exactly this reason, so a per-model rho here is the weaker form. s is exact (inverse-CDF CRN disagreement), so none of these numbers carries sampling error in the estimator itself.",
